\ ==== word locations in error traces ====

\ A colon word defined by load remembers its file and line; a runtime error
\ trace prints them after the word's name. REPL definitions carry none, and
\ forget drops the record with the word.

": faulty | x | x 0 / ;
: caller faulty 1 + ;
: chain caller ;" "/tmp/docs-trace.telic" write-file
"/tmp/docs-trace.telic" load

\ -- the trace names each loaded word with its file and line (chain tail-calls
\ caller, so its frame is gone by the time the error fires)
[: 5 chain :] [: :trace @ . cr :] try-catch
\ in / ← faulty (/tmp/docs-trace.telic:1) ← caller (/tmp/docs-trace.telic:2) ← try-catch

\ -- a REPL definition calling a loaded word: only the loaded frame is located
\ (top-level tail-calls faulty, so its own frame is already gone)
: top-level 7 faulty ;
[: top-level :] [: :trace @ . cr :] try-catch
\ in / ← faulty (/tmp/docs-trace.telic:1) ← top-level ← try-catch

\ -- forget drops the location with the word; a redefinition at the REPL has none
forget faulty
: faulty | x | x 0 / ;
[: 3 faulty :] [: :trace @ . cr :] try-catch
\ in / ← faulty ← try-catch

\ -- a definition through evaluate inside a loaded file has no location either
""": via-evaluate 1 0 / ;"" evaluate" "/tmp/docs-trace2.telic" write-file
"/tmp/docs-trace2.telic" load
[: via-evaluate :] [: :trace @ . cr :] try-catch
\ in / ← via-evaluate ← try-catch

clear
\ === an uncaught error prints the located trace on stderr ===
"/tmp/docs-trace.telic" load
2 chain

clear
\ === the line is the call executing in the frame, not the definition's line ===
\ (call-multi does not end in the call: as a tail call its frame would be gone)
": multi | x |
  x 1 +
  x 0 /
  drop ;
: call-multi
  1 2 +
  multi
  drop ;" "/tmp/docs-trace3.telic" write-file
"/tmp/docs-trace3.telic" load
[: 4 call-multi :] [: :trace @ . cr :] try-catch
\ in / ← multi (/tmp/docs-trace3.telic:3) ← call-multi (/tmp/docs-trace3.telic:7) ← try-catch
